"# T-101 AI-ranked search: recommendation to the review board\n\n**Decision: iterate.** The AI ranking raises orders among customers who search, but it slows the search screen\nbeyond the limit we set before the test: p95 latency is 405 ms worse against a limit of 300 ms. The\npre-registered rule says iterate: fix the latency, then confirm with a short test.\n\n## Effect\nOrders per triggered customer over 28 days rose by 0.226 (95% interval 0.179 to 0.273),\nthat is +3.5%, adjusted for each customer's orders in the 28 days before the test. Triggered customers\nare the 85,080 of 200,000 assigned customers who searched at least once.\n\n## Guardrails\n| Guardrail | Value | Limit | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| search latency p95 (ms), treatment minus control | 405 | at most 300 | FAILS |\n| inference cost per 1,000 searches (SAR), treatment | 5.801 | at most 8.0 | holds |\n| complaints per 1,000 orders, upper 95% bound of the difference | 0.362 | at most 1.0 | holds |\n| revenue per triggered customer, lower 95% bound of the relative difference | 0.016 | at least -0.01 | holds |\n\n## Risks and what would change this\n- The latency is a tail problem (the median rose far less); a faster model or a cache may fix it without losing the\n  lift. If latency cannot come under the limit, the decision becomes stop.\n- Complaints did not rise beyond the margin; the harm guardrail holds.\n- The sample ratio check holds (p = 0.06), and the trigger rate is equal in both arms (Lab 1.1).\n\n## Next step\nOwner: Yazeed Al-Hamdan. Reduce p95 latency by at least 105 ms, then run a 14-day confirmation\nwith the same metrics and rule.\n"
